# 09 - the descriptor the core is given is the open tunnel, and the library proves it
#
# After 08 the core stopped refusing the document and refused the descriptor instead: one
# start said `app/proxyman/inbound: failed to start proxy > inappropriate ioctl for device`
# while the start before it had resolved the tun's own name, so the fd path itself works on
# this platform — proxy/tun/tun_android.go takes the number from `xray.tun.fd`, sets it
# non-blocking, duplicates it and never ioctls it, which is why the two failures differ. What
# had gone wrong was the number: switching modes tears the VPN down and establishes it again,
# the service closes the descriptor of the tunnel it replaced, and the plugin kept handing that
# number over. A closed ParcelFileDescriptor reads -1, the library unsets `xray.tun.fd` for a
# non-positive number, proxy/tun falls back to 0, and the TUNGETIFF it runs on stdin is the
# `inappropriate ioctl for device` above. Three changes, one per place the number can go
# wrong: the plugin reads the descriptor once, refuses anything below 3 (0..2 are the standard
# streams) and drops a cached tunnel that closed instead of reusing it, waiting for the service
# to publish the one that replaced it; the service takes a previously published tunnel down
# before publishing a new one, so the plugin can never hold a live fd for an interface the
# system no longer routes into; and the library checks the descriptor it is handed with
# TUNGETIFF and Fstat before it starts the core, refusing a tap or a virtio-header tun and
# naming the number in logcat under the `libxray` tag. The fd path is proven, not assumed: the
# alternative the caller offered (socks inbound plus a forwarder) is not needed.

